\label{second_bound_pres}
Let $\Pi$ be a minimal vertex cutset separating a vertex $o$ from $\infty$, and let $A$ be the connected component of $o$ in $G \setminus \Pi$. If $\varnothing \neq S \subseteq A$ and every $\pi \in \Pi$ has a neighbour in $S$, then
$$\partial_\infty^V S = \Pi.$$

\label{markov}
Suppose that $(X_t)_{t\in \mathbb{N}_0}$ is transient and let $\varphi$ be the GFF on $G$ associated to $(X_t)_{t\in \mathbb{N}_0}$. Let $\Lambda \subset V$ be finite and non-empty. Then we may write
\begin{equation}\label{eq:markov}
\varphi = \varphi_{\Lambda} + h_\Lambda ,
\end{equation}
where $\varphi_{\Lambda}$ is a centred GFF on $V \setminus \Lambda$ with zero boundary conditions on $\Lambda$, which is independent of $\sigma(\varphi(v):v \in \Lambda)$. Moreover, $h_\Lambda$ is measurable with respect to $\sigma(\varphi(v) : v \in \Lambda)$ and is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:hK}
h_\Lambda(x) = \mathbb{E}^X_x\big(\varphi(X_{T_\Lambda}) \mathbf{1}_{\{T_\Lambda < \infty\}}\big) = \sum_{v \in \Lambda} \mathbb{P}^X_x\big(T_\Lambda < \infty,\, X_{T_\Lambda} = v\big)\, \varphi(v)
\end{equation}
for every $x \in V$ \footnote{Although it is easy to verify that this expression for $h_\Lambda(x)$ is indeed harmonic off $\Lambda$ and agrees with $\varphi$ on $\Lambda$, such an expression need not be unique. For example, on $\mathbb{Z}^3$ with $\Lambda = \{0\}$ and prescribed value $1$ at the origin, both $h \equiv 1$ and $h(x) = \mathbb{P}_x(T_0 < \infty)$ are harmonic off $\Lambda$ and equal to $1$ on $\Lambda$.}.

Let $\omega$ be Bernoulli-$p$ site percolation on $G$, and let $\eta\in\{0,1\}^{V/H}$ be defined by taking $\eta(u)=1$ if and only if there is at least one $\omega$-open vertex in the preimage $\tau^{-1}(u)$. For each vertex $v$ of $G$, we clearly have that the image under $\tau$ of the cluster of $v$ in $\omega$ is contained in the cluster of $\tau(v)$ in $\eta$. Moreover, the vertices of $G/H$ are open or closed in $\eta$ independently of one another and each is open with probability at most $q:=1-(1-p)^k$.

Let $(U(u))_{u\in V/H}$ be i.i.d.\ Uniform$[0,1]$ random variables with joint law $\mathbb{P}$, and let \linebreak${p_u:=\mathbb{P}^G_p(\eta(u)=1)}$. Define $\zeta(u):=\mathbf{1}_{\{U(u)<q\}}$; then $\zeta$ is Bernoulli-$q$ site percolation on $G/H$. Also define $\tilde\eta(u):=\mathbf{1}_{\{U(u)<p_u\}}$; since the $\tilde\eta(u)$ are independent across $u$ and each is Bernoulli-$p_u$, $\tilde\eta$ under law $\mathbb{P}$ has the same distribution as $\eta$ under law $\mathbb{P}_p^G$. Since $p_u\le q$, we have $\{U(u)<p_u\}\subseteq\{U(u)<q\}$, so $\tilde\eta\leq\zeta$ pointwise. Since $\tau(C_v)\subseteq C_{\tau(v)}$ and $A$ is increasing, 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{P}^G_p(\tau(C_v)\in A)\leq\mathbb{P}^G_p(C_{\tau(v)}(\eta)\in A) &= \mathbb{P}(C_{\tau(v)}(\tilde\eta)\in A) \\ &\leq\mathbb{P}(C_{\tau(v)}(\zeta)\in A) = \mathbb{P}^{G/H}_{1-(1-p)^k}\!\left(C_{\tau(v)} \in A\right). \qedhere
\end{align*}
